31:However, these results stand in contrast to studies that use alternative indices of variability. The Person-Based Similarity Index (PBSI) yields a personalised metric representing inter-subject correlations of neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les [34 -36], and has received recent attention in the context of psychiatric samples, including individuals with bipolar disorder [35, 36] and schizophrenia [36]. The PBSI was recently compared between CHR-P ( n = 71), FEP ( n = 72) and HC ( n = 55) [37], revealing heterogeneity at a personalised level in CHR-P samples. Further, those demonstrating the most marked deviation also demonstrated generally lower IQ and poorer psychopathology [37]. These /uniFB01 ndings are in contrast with the former meta-analytic /uniFB01 ndings [33]. However, these incongruities may be explained by the discrepant indices applied, the narrow focus of the brain regions studied meta-analytically [33] and/or the relatively small sample recruited for the PBSI investigations [37]. Taken together, the existing literature offers an ambiguous picture of neuroanatomical heterogeneity in the CHRP state; as such, further investigations are warranted.
99:Person-Based Similarity Index . The personalised estimates of interindividual variability were investigated using the PBSI, calculated according to the formula below, for each SA, CT and SV pro /uniFB01 les [34 -37]. The process for calculating the PBSI scores begins with concatenating the respective regional measures into vectors that represent the pro /uniFB01 le of each speci /uniFB01 c brain phenotype; PBSI-SA, PBSI-CT and PBSI-SV, respectively. This produces a simpli /uniFB01 ed, personalised index for each phenotypic neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le. This index can then be used in one of two ways; /uniFB01 rst, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is to that of other individuals with the same clinical pro /uniFB01 le or disorder (within-diagnosis or within-group). Second, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is respective to a normative estimate, i.e. the average of the healthy control group (normativeness) [37].
132:## Person-Based Similarity Index
140:We conducted a large-scale investigation of neuroanatomical heterogeneity in a help-seeking population meeting CHR-P criteria. To summarise, we observed a trend towards regional heterogeneity (as measured by the variability ratio) in a cluster of frontal, temporal and hippocampal regions that failed to reach statistical signi /uniFB01 cance after correction for multiple comparisons. However, Person-Based Similarity Index (PBSI) analyses, a novel measure of inter-individual variability, indicated greater divergence in global neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les of SA, CT and SV in CHRP compared with HC. Importantly, however, the proportion of CHR-P individuals with signi /uniFB01 cantly deviant PBSI scores was low. Moreover, none of the variability metrics examined showed signi /uniFB01 cant associations with a transition to psychosis.
322:Fig. 4 Violin plots comparing the distribution of PBSI scores between individuals at CHR-P and healthy controls, across surface area (PBSI\_SA), cortical thickness (PBSI\_CT), and subcortical volume (PBSI\_SV); the mid-point indicates the group mean. PBSI Person-Based Similarity Index, SA surface area, CT cortical thickness, SV subcortical volume. All three phenotypes demonstrate signi /uniFB01 cantly lower similarity in PBSI pro /uniFB01 les in the CHR-P group compared with healthy controls, across PBSI\_SA ( p &lt; 0.01), PBSI\_CT ( p &lt; 0.01), PBSI\_SV ( p &lt; 0.01).

